+\section{Engineering Experience and Scope}
+\label{sec:experience}
+The following lessons come from implementation and controlled experiments on
+three research machines. They do not establish production adoption.
+\todo{Add actual adoption context, duration, developer workflows, profiling
+sessions, maintenance burden, and resolved/unresolved user issues when available.}
+
+\subsection{Lessons from Backend Integration}
+\paragraph{The logger has its own execution mapping.}
+A mathematically correct kernel can publish an invalid trace. An early GCU
+logger combined a single selected writer with a vector store whose lowering
+distributed record words across work-items. Selecting one work-item left part
+of the record unwritten. Inspecting the lowered stores exposed the mismatch.
+The scalar publisher and explicit vector alternative consequently require
+validation of the lowered writer ownership and have different costs. The
+alternative repeats a complete logical record per work-item before selecting
+the writer. Trace validation must cover the actual writers and committed
+record bytes as well as numerical outputs.
+
+\paragraph{Association must survive runtime boundaries.}
+The Ascend launch caller and SDK worker can be different threads; caller-side
+last-task queries therefore do not establish the launched task's identity.
+The retained worker receipt crosses this boundary. A second boundary appears
+inside the native output: a mixed dispatch has both dispatch and compute
+context views. Their shared stream/task fields are insufficient to choose
+one view. Context membership and the SDK compute-task reference distinguish
+them while retaining both originals. Finally, temporary SDK output must
+outlive native finalization so Python can construct and retain this evidence.
+These failures arose at different boundaries even though all affected the
+same user-visible task association.
+
+\paragraph{Bounded recording is not a bound on perturbation.}
+Small output and selected instances do not establish low cost. GCU all-site
+Softmax instrumentation initially took about 4.65$\times$ the native device
+task time. The later one-store scalar experiment has much lower device
+perturbation, but detailed host submission remains about 5.2\,ms. NVIDIA site
+filtering and Ascend program publication predicates also have distinct
+measurement boundaries: discarded output does not imply that counter reads
+or instrumentation were absent. Recording limits and cost must be evaluated
+separately.
+
+\subsection{Scope and Threats to Validity}
+Device comparisons include compiler and SDK differences; they do not isolate
+microarchitecture as the sole cause of performance differences. The operator
+matrix and preselected held-out task exercise a finite set of shapes and
+configurations. They do not establish application-level speedups or arbitrary
+compiler-transform support. Missing source origins remain partial, and local
+duration conversion does not establish cross-actor epoch alignment. Native
+task envelopes, source-control intervals, and independently replayed counters
+have different scopes. NVIDIA performance remains pending a suitable
+measurement window; its functional tests do not resolve that limitation.
+\todo{Update these bounds from final fidelity tests and the independent review;
+do not replace missing measurements or operational experience with engineering
+test counts.}
